  "topic": "Symbol Resolution",
  "explanation": "Symbol resolution is the process of associating symbolic names (like function or variable names used in source code) with their actual memory addresses during the linking and loading of a program.  This is crucial because the compiler and assembler work with symbolic names, but the processor needs absolute memory addresses to execute instructions.  The linker resolves these symbols by matching references to symbols in one object file with their definitions in other object files, or in shared libraries.  Without proper symbol resolution, the program cannot run correctly because references would point to incorrect memory locations.",
  "syntax": "The specific syntax varies depending on the linker and operating system (e.g., ld on Linux, link on Windows).  Generally, symbol resolution involves the linker scanning object files and shared libraries for symbols referenced in a program but not yet defined. It resolves these references by matching them to symbol definitions found elsewhere.  The resulting executable or shared library contains the resolved addresses.",
