  "example_explanation": "This example demonstrates symbol resolution.  'main.c' calls 'myFunction', which is defined in 'mylib.c'. The compiler generates object files for each source file. During the linking stage (using GCC here), the linker resolves the symbol 'myFunction'. It finds the definition of 'myFunction' in the object file compiled from 'mylib.c' and replaces the symbolic reference in 'main.c' with the actual memory address where 'myFunction' is located in the final executable 'myprogram'.  If the linker failed to resolve 'myFunction', a linker error would occur because the call in 'main.c' would point to an undefined address.",
